<theodulf. carm. 29.1>	Lege tonantis eri retegit qua crimina lator,
<theodulf. carm. 29.2>	Infaustum perimit insidiantis iter.
<theodulf. carm. 29.3>	Legis amator ovans multandos denique fures
<theodulf. carm. 29.4>	Rebus in eductis censuit esse quater.
<theodulf. carm. 29.5>	Tempore cum primo maneant praecepta severa,
<theodulf. carm. 29.6>	Temporibus nostris crudeliora manent.
<theodulf. carm. 29.7>	Cumque quater denis exactis ferme diebus
<theodulf. carm. 29.8>	Nilque cibi caperet, intonat ille polo:
<theodulf. carm. 29.9>	"Qui clepit arte bovem latitans iuvamine fretus,
<theodulf. carm. 29.10>	Restituat quinum, cui male dempsit opem;
<theodulf. carm. 29.11>	De gregibus rapuit pulchram qui perfidus agnam,
<theodulf. carm. 29.12>	Bis referat binam, cui male dempsit eam."
<theodulf. carm. 29.13>	Non capitis saevae capiat discrimina poenae,
<theodulf. carm. 29.14>	Non oculis careat nec furiosus ope.
<theodulf. carm. 29.15>	Ingenio lateat rapiens nisi tempore nostro,
<theodulf. carm. 29.16>	Conprensus noxae tollitur ecce nece,
<theodulf. carm. 29.17>	Nec niveis poterit fulgenti vita metallo
<theodulf. carm. 29.18>	Eripier nummis, qui clepit ecce modo,
<theodulf. carm. 29.19>	Aurea nec poterunt tanto discrimine dona
<theodulf. carm. 29.20>	Eximere oblata, qui modo demit ovem;
<theodulf. carm. 29.21>	Nec redolens, flagrat muris quae tergore, vestis
<theodulf. carm. 29.22>	Aut fucata nitens, quas habet ater Arabs,
<theodulf. carm. 29.23>	Nec capitis rutilans, pulchris distincta metallis,
<theodulf. carm. 29.24>	Neve corona potest esse salutis opus.
<theodulf. carm. 29.25>	Praedia nec fundi, famulus nec morte relictus
<theodulf. carm. 29.26>	Funera post patris causa salutis adest:
<theodulf. carm. 29.27>	Quin oculos, generis pulcri stirpemque parentis,
<theodulf. carm. 29.28>	Crusque manusque simul iura moderna levant.
<theodulf. carm. 29.29>	Terga iubent flagris caedi, os perfundere plumbo,
<theodulf. carm. 29.30>	Abscidique aures, naris et omne decus,
<theodulf. carm. 29.31>	Et celeres truncare pedes et vincula collo
<theodulf. carm. 29.32>	A trabe sublimi ferre clepentis onus.
<theodulf. carm. 29.33>	Iura poli reboant: "Animae sublator humanae
<theodulf. carm. 29.34>	Pro vita vitam ponat iniquus atram.
<theodulf. carm. 29.35>	Sic oculos oculis reddat, sic auribus aures,
<theodulf. carm. 29.36>	Pro manibusque manus, pro pedibusque pedes.
<theodulf. carm. 29.37>	Urit ut insontem, sontem fac ignis adurat,
<theodulf. carm. 29.38>	Livorem livor inferat atque sibi.
<theodulf. carm. 29.39>	Bucula pro bove cogatur bis bina referri,
<theodulf. carm. 29.40>	Addita rapta simul constitit una cui;
<theodulf. carm. 29.41>	Singula cui furto sublataque ovicula constat,
<theodulf. carm. 29.42>	Bina referre iubet, demat ut omne nefas."
<theodulf. carm. 29.43>	Haec benedicta boat legis censura tonantis,
<theodulf. carm. 29.44>	Haec retinenda iubet et facienda monet.
<theodulf. carm. 29.45>	Omnes diverso nunc currunt tramite leges,
<theodulf. carm. 29.46>	Nescio, sint leges an mage sitque furor.
<theodulf. carm. 29.47>	Sanguine pro porci fuso datur immo caput iam,
<theodulf. carm. 29.48>	Pro pecude humanus funditur ecce cruor.
<theodulf. carm. 29.49>	Hirsutam rapiat si forsan quisque capellam,
<theodulf. carm. 29.50>	Stipite suspensus excruciatus obit.
<theodulf. carm. 29.51>	Si furibundus atrox homines percusserit amens,
<theodulf. carm. 29.52>	Et calor exanima menbra relinquat ebes,
<theodulf. carm. 29.53>	Vile datur pretium tanti pro crimine facti
<theodulf. carm. 29.54>	Aut nummi aut pecudis aut rude saepe metum.
<theodulf. carm. 29.55>	Cara creatori maneant cum cuncta creata,
<theodulf. carm. 29.56>	Carior in cunctis, crede, creatus homo:
<theodulf. carm. 29.57>	Nec vili pretio pecudis nec aequus haberi
<theodulf. carm. 29.58>	Celsior in cunctis iste putandus erit.
<theodulf. carm. 29.59>	Lex antiqua dei veteres, sic probra recidens,
<theodulf. carm. 29.60>	Tum docuit homines et pius ordo dedit,
<theodulf. carm. 29.61>	Iamque novo veteres pepulerunt lumine sordes
<theodulf. carm. 29.62>	Tensa creatoris stipite membra crucis.
<theodulf. carm. 29.63>	Censuit ille pius rapientis vivere corpus,
<theodulf. carm. 29.64>	Et potius lacrimis sic scelus omne mori,
<theodulf. carm. 29.65>	Nec mala proque malis, crimen pro crimine dirum,
<theodulf. carm. 29.66>	Neve vicem laesum reddere cogit erus.
<theodulf. carm. 29.67>	Sed potiora iubet, saevis conferre benigna,
<theodulf. carm. 29.68>	Pro sermone nequam gratius esse iubet.
<theodulf. carm. 29.69>	Quattuor egregie reboant per compita mundi
<theodulf. carm. 29.70>	Haec proceres, quorum vox sonat una simul.
<theodulf. carm. 29.71>	Intonat emissa ingenti hoc turbine Pauli
<theodulf. carm. 29.72>	Vox tremebunda polo, perculit unde nefas.
<theodulf. carm. 29.73>	Sic ait exiguo prostratus corpore terra:
<theodulf. carm. 29.74>	"Proque malo nulli reddere cogo malum,
<theodulf. carm. 29.75>	Provideat potius vestra sollertia cunctis
<theodulf. carm. 29.76>	Quae iucunda manent, non nocitura ferat."
<theodulf. carm. 29.77>	Sic virtute nitens, fandi facundus in arte,
<theodulf. carm. 29.78>	Sic sermone potens mistica plura canit.
<theodulf. carm. 29.79>	O utinam nunc forte foret censura quietae
<theodulf. carm. 29.80>	Legis, ab aeterea quam dedit arce deus,
<theodulf. carm. 29.81>	Ingenio raptam pecudem vestemque superbam,
<theodulf. carm. 29.82>	Pro pecude ut pecudem reddat iniqua manus.
<theodulf. carm. 29.83>	Pro niveo vestis reddatur tegmine vestis,
<theodulf. carm. 29.84>	Quam rapuit saeva seu furiosa manus.
